[32m+[m[32m### Deploy separate services to Render[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe root `render.yaml` defines separate Render services for the Auth API, Fruit[m
[32m+[m[32mAPI, and frontend. Each API builds from its own Dockerfile while sharing the[m
[32m+[m[32mbackend requirements and common modules. Connect the repository to Render and[m
[32m+[m[32mcreate a Blueprint; Render will request the unsynced environment variables.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mSet `DATABASE_URL` on both APIs to the same hosted PostgreSQL connection string,[m
[32m+[m[32mand set `JWT_SECRET` to the same private random value on both services. Once[m
[32m+[m[32mRender assigns the frontend URL, set `CORS_ORIGINS` on both APIs to that exact[m
[32m+[m[32morigin (for example, `https://fruit-house-frontend.onrender.com`, without a[m
[32m+[m[32mtrailing slash). The frontend build variables must be the full API base URLs:[m
[32m+[m[32m`VITE_AUTH_API_URL=https://<auth-service>.onrender.com/api/auth` and[m
[32m+[m[32m`VITE_FRUIT_API_URL=https://<fruit-service>.onrender.com/api`. These values are[m
[32m+[m[32mbaked into the static frontend at build time, so redeploy it after changing[m
[32m+[m[32mthem. Each API has a `/health` endpoint for Render health checks.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mKeep the database URL, JWT secret, and any other credentials in Render's[m
[32m+[m[32menvironment settings; do not commit them to the repository. The services use[m
[32m+[m[32mRender's `PORT` at runtime and require no fixed public port configuration.[m
[32m+[m[32mProduct image uploads are written to the Fruit API filesystem. Render's[m
[32m+[m[32mcontainer filesystem is ephemeral by default. The Render Blueprint attaches a[m
[32m+[m[32m1 GB persistent disk at `/app/Fruit Content/Api/uploads` and uses the Starter[m
[32m+[m[32mweb-service plan for the Fruit API; review Render's current pricing before[m
[32m+[m[32mdeploying. The disk preserves uploaded files across deploys and restarts.[m
[32m+[m
 Both APIs use the same `JWT_SECRET` to sign and verify seven-day access tokens.[m
